---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="How to use Where to Apply · Tech job search guide" description="A practical guide to using Where to Apply to discover software, AI, and FDE roles in India and remote.">
  <main class="wrap py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link text-sm" href="/">← Where to Apply</a>
    <article class="mt-8 max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">A practical job search directory for tech roles</h1>
      <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-fg-2 leading-relaxed">
        <p>Where to Apply brings together independent job boards, startup career pages, venture capital portfolio listings, remote platforms, hiring communities, and people who share openings. Use the list to widen your search beyond the largest job sites, then follow each source's own application process. The role and city search builder creates targeted searches for common hiring platforms, while filters help you focus on India, remote work, AI, startups, or forward deployed engineering. The directory is maintained as a public resource and its links are checked regularly. It does not promise that a source has an active opening today; always confirm role details and eligibility on the destination site before applying.</p>
        <p>A useful search is usually a mix of broad and direct routes. Large boards help establish the range of roles and titles in the market. Startup boards and investor portfolio pages point toward smaller teams, where the company may publish its own application form. AI and FDE sources are included because those jobs can use titles such as applied AI engineer, solutions engineer, or customer engineer as well as the more familiar labels. Remote sources differ in the countries they can hire from, so check location restrictions before investing time in an application. The city pages preset the builder and list sources tagged for that location, while the category pages narrow the directory to a particular kind of source. These views are starting points, and they can be combined with the directory filters to fit a specific experience level, skill set, or search plan.</p>
        <p>Keep a simple record of where you have looked and when you last checked each listing. The checklist stays in your browser, and the shared filters are represented in the page URL so you can save or send a focused view. Before applying, confirm that a vacancy is current, read the employer's own description, and use the official application link where one is provided. For a referral or a direct message, keep the note specific: name the role, explain the relevant work you have done, and include one useful example. The templates and tips on the homepage are prompts to adapt, not messages to send unchanged. A thoughtful, accurate application helps both the candidate and hiring team make better use of their time.</p>
        <p>Start with the location and role that fit your search, then check several source types instead of relying on one feed. Company career pages and portfolio boards can surface openings that are not widely reposted. Community groups and hiring posts can help you learn who is building a team, while remote platforms clarify whether a company considers applicants based in India. Use the links here as leads, compare the original listing for freshness, and tailor your application to the employer's stated requirements. The directory is designed to make discovery easier; decisions about fit, eligibility, and whether to apply remain with the job seeker.</p>
        <p>This directory is especially useful for candidates who want to explore the Indian technology market, compare local and remote opportunities, or find teams working in AI and applied engineering. It includes familiar job sites as well as focused sources that are easy to miss, such as startup portfolios, community boards, and direct hiring posts. Use each listing as a lead and check the source itself for the latest role, location, and application details. You can return to the list, adjust a filter, and keep a browser-local checklist as your search evolves.</p>
        <p>The directory is organized around discovery rather than application tracking or recruitment. Sources link out to the organizations that publish jobs, and their descriptions explain what kind of lead each one offers. Some are broad boards with many listings; others focus on a region, a technology, a stage of company, or a specific community. A source can be valuable even when it does not have a matching role today, because it may help you understand which teams are active and where they announce new work. The directory is updated as links are reviewed and new recommendations are contributed. If a listing becomes outdated or a useful source is missing, use the repository issue tracker to share a correction or suggestion with the maintainer.</p>
        <p>Job titles vary between employers, particularly across AI and customer-facing engineering teams. A search for a single exact title can miss related work, so try adjacent terms that reflect the work you want to do. For example, an FDE search can be complemented with applied AI, solutions, implementation, or customer engineering terms. Likewise, remote does not always mean worldwide: companies may hire only in a set of countries or time zones. Check requirements carefully and use the search builder to open a few targeted queries, then inspect each employer's own listing for responsibilities, seniority, location, and application instructions.</p>
        <p>Browse the directory to compare sources, open focused category or city pages, and build searches for the roles you want. Each link leads to the original publisher so you can verify the latest information.</p>
        <p><a class="link" href="/">Browse the job search directory →</a></p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
